        <div id="two-vs-four" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 2-Cycle vs 4-Cycle Gas String Trimmers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best gas string trimmer for most homes is a 2-cycle model with a straight or curved shaft that matches your yard. Engine type is the first choice, and it decides how you fuel the trimmer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2-Cycle Trimmers</h3>

          <p>
            Every trimmer on this list is a 2-cycle (2-stroke) model. They are lighter and simpler than 4-cycle engines and produce plenty of power for their size. You mix 2-cycle oil into the gasoline at the ratio in the manual, usually 50:1, or buy pre-mixed canned fuel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4-Cycle Trimmers</h3>

          <p>
            4-cycle trimmers run on straight gasoline with oil in a separate crankcase, so there is no mixing. They are quieter but heavier and need oil changes, and fewer models are available.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, a 2-cycle trimmer is the practical choice: lighter, simpler to repair and widely supported. Mix fuel in small batches and add stabilizer if it will sit; see our guide to the <a href="/best-fuel-stabilizer/">best fuel stabilizers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

