          </h2>

          <p>
            Shaft shape matters more to how a trimmer feels than any engine spec.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Straight Shafts</h3>

          <p>
            Straight shafts, like the Husqvarna 330LK, 525L, Troy-Bilt TB25SB and ECHO SRM-2620, reach under shrubs, decks and fence rails, and suit taller users. They are usually more durable and are the choice for large properties.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Curved Shafts</h3>

          <p>
            Curved shafts, like the Husqvarna 130C, 122C and RYOBI, are shorter, lighter and easier to balance. They suit smaller yards and shorter users, and they are easier to control around beds and trees.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Attachment-Capable Shafts</h3>

          <p>
            The Husqvarna 330LK and RYOBI accept attachments such as edgers and pole saws. They make sense if you would otherwise buy several separate gas tools.
          </p>

