          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Engine Size: How Many cc Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas trimmer engines on this list range from 22cc to 28cc. More cc means more power, and more weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">22cc to 25cc</h3>

          <p>
            Engines like the Husqvarna 122C and Troy-Bilt TB25SB handle grass along edges, beds and walkways in typical yards. They are lighter and easier on the arms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">25cc to 28cc</h3>

          <p>
            The Husqvarna 525L, 130C and ECHO SRM-2620 have more power for thick grass, weeds and overgrown fence lines, and they spin heavier line more easily.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching Line to Engine</h3>

          <p>
            Bigger engines handle heavier line. The RYOBI runs dual 0.095-inch line, which lasts longer against tough weeds than thinner homeowner line. Use the line size your trimmer's manual specifies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas vs Battery String Trimmers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery trimmers have improved a lot, so it is worth asking whether you need gas at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Gas Still Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Gas trimmers run as long as you have fuel, handle thick growth and heavy line, and suit large properties, fence lines and acreage where a battery would need several swaps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Battery Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Battery trimmers are quieter, start instantly, need no fuel mixing and almost no maintenance. For a typical suburban yard, they are often the easier choice. See our guides to the <a href="/best-battery-string-trimmer/">best battery string trimmers</a> and the <a href="/best-string-trimmer/">best string trimmers</a> overall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deciding Question</h3>

          <p>
            If you trim for more than about 30 to 45 minutes at a time or cut heavy weeds, gas is usually the better tool. If your yard is small and quiet matters, consider battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Trimmer Heads and Line: Reloading Made Easier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reloading line is the job most people dislike, and head design makes a big difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tap and Bump Heads</h3>

          <p>
            Husqvarna's Tap N' Go and RYOBI's bump-feed heads release more line when you tap the head on the ground. They are the standard on homeowner trimmers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quick-Load Heads</h3>

          <p>
            The Husqvarna 330LK's Rapid Replace head reloads by aligning, loading and twisting, without opening the head. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-restring-a-weed-eater/">how to restring a weed eater</a> covers the standard method.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line Shape and Size</h3>

          <p>
            Round line lasts longest; twisted and shaped lines cut more cleanly. Heavier line, like 0.095-inch, resists breaking against fences and concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="starting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Start a Gas Trimmer and Keep It Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most gas trimmer problems come down to old fuel and starting technique.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Use Fresh, Correctly Mixed Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            Mix 2-cycle oil at the ratio in the manual using fresh gasoline, and do not keep mixed fuel for months. Stale fuel is the most common cause of hard starting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Purge, Choke and Pull</h3>

          <p>
            Press the purge bulb until fuel flows (Husqvarna's Air Purge), set the choke for a cold start, and pull steadily. Spring-assisted starters, like those on the 330LK and Troy-Bilt, make this easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Clean the Air Filter</h3>

          <p>
            A clogged air filter starves the engine. Check it every few uses in dusty conditions and clean or replace it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Store It Properly</h3>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, run the tank dry or use stabilized fuel. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-lawn-mower/">winterizing a lawn mower</a> covers the same small-engine routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety Gear for Gas Trimmers
          </h2>

          <p>
            String trimmers throw debris at high speed. Basic gear prevents most injuries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Eyes and Ears</h3>

          <p>
            Wear safety glasses or a face shield every time. Gas trimmers are loud, so hearing protection is wise on longer jobs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Legs and Feet</h3>

          <p>
            Long trousers and sturdy closed shoes protect against stones and line. Keep people and pets well back while you work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shoulder Straps</h3>

          <p>
            A shoulder strap spreads the weight of heavier straight-shaft trimmers and keeps the head at the right height, reducing fatigue and improving control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attachments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Attachment-Capable Trimmers: One Engine, Several Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            An attachment-capable trimmer can replace several separate gas tools, if you choose the system carefully.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Common Attachments</h3>

          <p>
            Edgers, pole saws, hedge trimmers, cultivators and blowers are the usual add-ons. The RYOBI accepts universal brand-fit attachments, and the Husqvarna 330LK takes click-on tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Saves Money</h3>

          <p>
            If you would otherwise buy a separate <a href="/best-pole-saw/">pole saw</a> and <a href="/best-hedge-trimmer/">hedge trimmer</a>, one engine with attachments saves money and storage space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Buy Separate Tools</h3>

          <p>
            Swapping attachments takes time, and a dedicated tool is often better balanced. If you use one tool far more than the others, a dedicated model may suit you better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="edging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Edging With a Gas String Trimmer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A string trimmer can give driveways and walkways a crisp edge without a separate edger, once you learn the technique.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turn the Trimmer on Its Side</h3>

          <p>
            Rotate the trimmer so the head spins vertically along the edge of the concrete. Walk slowly and let the line cut a clean vertical line in the turf. A straight shaft, like the Husqvarna 525L or Troy-Bilt TB25SB, makes this easier to control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expect Faster Line Wear</h3>

          <p>
            Edging against concrete wears line quickly. Heavier line, like the RYOBI's 0.095-inch, lasts longer, and tap-feed heads make it quick to advance more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Dedicated Edger Is Better</h3>

          <p>
            For long driveways edged every week, an edger attachment on an attachment-capable trimmer, like the Husqvarna 330LK or RYOBI, gives a cleaner, deeper cut with less effort.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thick-weeds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Trimming Thick Weeds and Light Brush
          </h2>

          <p>
            Overgrown fence lines and ditches are where gas trimmers earn their keep, but technique and expectations matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Power You Have</h3>

          <p>
            Larger engines, like the Husqvarna 130C's 28cc, spin the line hard enough for thick, tall weeds. Work from the top down in stages rather than trying to cut tall growth at the base in one pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Narrow the Cut for Power</h3>

          <p>
            The Husqvarna 330LK lets you narrow its cut from 20 to 18 inches for more power in thick vegetation, which is the right setting for overgrown areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know the Limit</h3>

          <p>
            String is for grass and weeds. Woody brush and saplings need a brush cutter blade or a different tool entirely; forcing line through them wears it out and strains the engine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. How to Trim Like a Pro
          </h2>

          <p>
            Good technique makes any trimmer faster and gives a neater result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Hold the Head Slightly Tilted</h3>

          <p>
            Tilt the head a few degrees so only the tip of the line does the cutting. The tip moves fastest and cuts most cleanly, and the line lasts longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Cut in the Direction Debris Will Go</h3>

          <p>
            Most heads, including Husqvarna's, spin counterclockwise and throw debris to one side. Work so clippings fly away from you, the house and the driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Work at Mower Height</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the line at the same height as your mower deck so trimmed areas blend with the lawn rather than leaving scalped strips.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Slow Down Around Trees</h3>

          <p>
            Line strikes damage young bark. Trim slowly around trees and posts, or use mulch rings so you do not need to trim right against the trunk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="season-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Gas Trimmer Maintenance Through the Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few minutes of care at the right times keeps a gas trimmer starting first pull for years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start of the Season</h3>

          <p>
            Fit a fresh spark plug if the old one is worn or fouled, clean or replace the air filter, and fill with freshly mixed fuel. Check the trimmer head for cracks and load new line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">During the Season</h3>

          <p>
            Clean grass and sap off the head and shield after each use, tap dust out of the air filter every few sessions, and tighten any loose screws on the guard and handle. Vibration loosens hardware over time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">End of the Season</h3>

          <p>
            Run the engine until it stops so no fuel is left in the carburetor, or fill with stabilized fuel. Store it dry and hang it up rather than leaving it on a concrete floor, which keeps the line and shield from being bent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Get It Serviced</h3>

          <p>
            If the engine surges, stalls under load or will not idle after fresh fuel and a clean filter, the carburetor may need cleaning. A small-engine shop can usually fix this quickly, and it is far cheaper than replacing the trimmer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Stale or Wrongly Mixed Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old fuel and the wrong oil ratio cause most starting and running problems in 2-cycle trimmers. Mix fresh fuel in small batches at the ratio in the manual, and stabilize it if it will sit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Curved Shaft for a Big Property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Curved shafts are comfortable in small yards but reach poorly under shrubs and along long fence lines. For larger properties, a straight shaft like the Husqvarna 330LK or 525L saves time and stooping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Wrong Line Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Line that is too heavy strains a small engine; line that is too light breaks constantly. Use the diameter your manual specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Eye Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Trimmers throw stones and debris hard enough to injure eyes. Wear safety glasses or a face shield every time, even for a quick tidy-up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Fuel in the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fuel left in the tank and carburetor over winter varnishes and blocks small passages. Run it dry or use stabilized fuel before storage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

