        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Husqvarna 330LK is the best gas string trimmer for larger yards: an adjustable 18 or 20-inch cut, attachment capability, a Rapid Replace head and an easier spring-assisted start. The Husqvarna 525L is the best simpler straight shaft, the 130C the best curved shaft, and the Troy-Bilt TB25SB the best budget pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a straight or curved shaft weed eater better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Straight shafts reach under shrubs, decks and fence rails better and suit taller users and larger properties. Curved shafts are lighter, shorter and easier to balance, which suits smaller yards and shorter users. If you trim long fence lines or under bushes, choose straight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a 2-cycle and 4-cycle trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2-cycle trimmer runs on gasoline mixed with 2-cycle oil and is lighter and simpler. A 4-cycle trimmer runs on straight gasoline with separate engine oil, so there is no mixing, but it is heavier and needs oil changes. Every trimmer in this guide is 2-cycle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas trimmer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For edges, beds and walkways in a typical yard, a 22cc to 25cc engine like the Husqvarna 122C or Troy-Bilt TB25SB is enough. For thick weeds, overgrown fence lines and larger properties, choose 25cc to 28cc, like the Husqvarna 525L, 130C or ECHO SRM-2620.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gas string trimmers better than battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas trimmers are better for long sessions, thick growth and large properties, because they run as long as you have fuel. Battery trimmers are quieter, start instantly and need almost no maintenance, which suits typical suburban yards. Choose by yard size and how long you trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why won't my gas trimmer start?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common cause is stale or incorrectly mixed fuel. Drain old fuel, refill with fresh gasoline mixed at the ratio in your manual, purge the bulb and use the choke for a cold start. A clogged air filter or fouled spark plug are the next things to check.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What fuel does a gas string trimmer use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                2-cycle trimmers use gasoline mixed with 2-cycle engine oil, usually at 50:1; check your manual for the exact ratio. Use fresh fuel, avoid storing mixed fuel for months, and add fuel stabilizer if it will sit. Pre-mixed canned fuel is a convenient alternative.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

